<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="assimilatory sulfate reduction II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="SULFMETII-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Sulfur is an essential nutrient for all life forms. Plants, fungi, and many bacteria reduce inorganic |FRAME: SULFATE| to sulfide to cover their need for the element. Before the sulfur can be assimilated into biosynthetic pathways it needs to be reduced to |FRAME:HS|. Pathways catalyzing the reduction of sulfate to sulfide for the purpose of incorporation into newly synthesized molecules are called pathways of &lt;b&gt;assimilatory sulfate reduction&lt;/b&gt;. In addition, certain organisms can use sulfate as an alternative electron acceptor in the absence of oxygen. Pathways of sulfate reduction for the purpose of energy production are called pathways of &lt;b&gt;dissimilatory sulfate reduction&lt;/b&gt;. Sulfate can not be reduced to sulfide in a single step, and is first reduced to |FRAME: SO3|. However, two electrons required to reduce of sulfate to sulfite have a standard redox potential (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&apos;&lt;/i&gt;) of -516 mV, which is too high for physiological electron carriers |CITS: [860983]|. The problem is solved by the activation of the sulfate by phosphorylation, forming a mixed anhydride between phosphate and sulfate as in |FRAME:APS| (APS) or |FRAME:PAPS| (PAPS). This linkage lowers the potential of the electrons to &lt;i&gt;E&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&apos;&lt;/i&gt; = -60 mV, which is easilly covered by thiols or pyrimidine nucleotides |CITS: [14627706]|. In plants, as well as in certain bacteria (such as |FRAME: TAX-1049| |CITS: [10939523]|), |FRAME:APS APS| is reduced directly to sulfite. In enteric bacteria, cyanobacteria, and yeast, on the other hand, |FRAME:APS APS| is further phophorylated to |FRAME:PAPS PAPS|, and only the latter is reduced to sulfite (see |FRAME: SO4ASSIM-PWY|) |CITS: [11940598]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Even though this pathway was intially described in plants, it has been shown to exist in some bacteria, such as |FRAME: TAX-1049| |CITS: [10939523]|. As described above, the initial step of sulfate reduction is its activation to |FRAME: APS| (APS). This phosphorylation is performed by the enzyme |FRAME: EC-2.7.7.4| (often known as ATP sulfurylase). It should be noted that in some sulfur-oxidizing bacteria, such as |FRAME: TAX-1049|, there are two two such enzymes - an |FRAME: CPLX-8836| and a |FRAME: CPLX-8194|. Only the latter is relevant for this pathway. Two types of enzymes are known to catalyze the second step - the direct reduction of APS to sulfite - |FRAME:EC-1.8.4.9| and |FRAME: EC-1.8.4.10|. This pathway describes organisms that posses the former. The latter enzyme is described in |FRAME: PWY-6683|. Once formed, sulfite can reduced to sulfide by the action of multiple enzymes. While most bacteria utilze NADPH as an electron donor (|FRAME: EC-1.8.7.2|), the plant sulfite reductases, as well as the enzyme from |FRAME: TAX-1049|, use ferredoxin for this purpose (|FRAME: EC-1.8.7.1|). In bacteria and plants, sulfide is finally incorporated into |FRAME: ACETYLSERINE| by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.47| yielding |FRAME: CYS|, and later into |FRAME: MET|, which are the two major sulfur-containing precursor compounds (see |FRAME: CYSTSYN-PWY|). Earlier literature, describing work mostly performed with the alga |FRAME: ORG-6016|, describes a different sulfate reduction pathway in plants, involving thiol-bound sulfite intermediates |CITS: [16655178][16655893][16656806][4788946][398767]|. However, the purification and characterization of |FRAME:CPLX-7175| from |FRAME: TAX-4472| revealed that the only product of the enzyme is indeed free sulfite, and that the thiol-bound intermediates were most likely artefacts due to the presence of oxidized thiols in the reaction media |CITS: [10625629]|. &lt;B&gt;Regulation of the pathway in plants&lt;/B&gt; Sulfate assimilation in plants is regulated by several factors. (i) Developmental stage: the highest activities of sulfate assimilation enzymes are found in growing tissues including young leaves and root tips where there is a high demand for cysteine and metionine for protein biosynthesis. (ii) Availability of reductants: two of the enzymes in this pathway, APS reductase and sulfite reductase, require reductants (glutathione and ferredoxin, respectively) to remain active. In addition, the availability of reductants is related to the availability of light, the ultimate electron source in photosynthetic tissues. In leaves, the activity of sulfite reductase was shown to be directly linked to circadian phasing. (iii) Sulfur availability: gene expression of sulfate assimilation enzymes was induced by sulfur deficiency conditions |CITS: [9380766]|. (iv) Stress: gene expression was also found to be induced by exposing plants to heavy metals and oxidative stress |CITS: [9380766]|. More information is available in |CITS: [leustek02]| and |CITS:[16307301]|.</Description>
